how_is_this_frame_animal_well_rendered"},{"short_id":"eqc1s5","created_at":"2026-09-05T17:23:45.680-05:00","title":"Controlling when CSS custom property values are computed","url":"https://jakearchibald.com/2026/css-custom-property-compute-time/","score":3,"flags":0,"comment_count":0,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"typesanitizer","user_is_author":false,"tags":["css"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/eqc1s5","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/eqc1s5/controlling_when_css_custom_property"},{"short_id":"xnqxqe","created_at":"2026-09-05T16:28:47.233-05:00","title":"The revolt of the reader","url":"https://bcantrill.dtrace.org/2026/09/05/the-revolt-of-the-reader/","score":106,"flags":0,"comment_count":14,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"prattmic","user_is_author":false,"tags":["practices","vibecoding"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/xnqxqe","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/xnqxqe/revolt_reader"},{"short_id":"qnnvq2","created_at":"2026-09-05T16:08:36.515-05:00","title":"Large-Language Models as a Cognitive Virus","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.03344","score":14,"flags":1,"comment_count":2,"description":"\u003cp\u003eLarge-language models (LLMs) are rapidly becoming part of human culture, reshaping how information is produced, transmitted, and used. Here we propose that their diffusion can be understood through a viral analogy, with LLM use spreading through populations, becoming embedded in cognitive and cultural practices. We model transitions among uncoupled, coupled, and persistently dependent users, and show that the interplay between social transmission, recovery, and collective reinforcement can generate tipping points and technological lock-in. A central consequence is the possibility of runaway dynamics: once a critical threshold is crossed, small increases in adoption can trigger rapid population-level shifts toward persistent dependence, with abrupt losses in cognitive competence. The same framework, however, identifies conditions for cognitive immunization, based on reducing transmission and facilitating reversibility. Our results highlight how LLM adoption may involve nonlinear collective transitions with important consequences for cognitive autonomy.\u003c/p\u003e\n","description_plain":"Large-language models (LLMs) are rapidly becoming part of human culture, reshaping how information is produced, transmitted, and used. Here we propose that their diffusion can be understood through a viral analogy, with LLM use spreading through populations, becoming embedded in cognitive and cultural practices. We model transitions among uncoupled, coupled, and persistently dependent users, and show that the interplay between social transmission, recovery, and collective reinforcement can generate tipping points and technological lock-in. A central consequence is the possibility of runaway dynamics: once a critical threshold is crossed, small increases in adoption can trigger rapid population-level shifts toward persistent dependence, with abrupt losses in cognitive competence. The same framework, however, identifies conditions for cognitive immunization, based on reducing transmission and facilitating reversibility. Our results highlight how LLM adoption may involve nonlinear collective transitions with important consequences for cognitive autonomy.","submitter_user":"Corbin","user_is_author":false,"tags":["cogsci","pdf","vibecoding","visualization"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/qnnvq2","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/qnnvq2/large_language_models_as_cognitive_virus"},{"short_id":"f2ele4","created_at":"2026-09-05T13:46:39.834-05:00","title":"C++26: std::hive","url":"https://www.sandordargo.com/blog/2026/09/02/cpp26-hive","score":5,"flags":2,"comment_count":1,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"raymii","user_is_author":false,"tags":["c++"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/f2ele4","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/f2ele4/c_26_std_hive"}